From b5bb2559b72fb48ef14cf4f33fe8730511fd0222 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:39:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, windowing/gbm: upscalers for videos drawn larger
 than they are, off by default
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A video smaller than the screen is scaled up by Kodi with one filter
(bilinear by default, Lanczos 3 or others by choice). mpv's prescalers
rebuild the luma at twice its size from what they learned or measured
of real pictures, and come closer to the original than any filter:
measured on film, animation and HDR10 pictures scaled down 2 or 3
times and up again (PSNR of Y', mean over the frames, against
Lanczos 3), RAVU
lite r4 gains 0.1 to 1.3 dB for 2 to 3 times the GPU time of Lanczos,
FSRCNNX 8 gains 0.3 to 3.4 dB for 5 to 13 times, and FSRCNNX 16 gains
0.4 to 4.7 dB for 14 to 38 times. Their mean colour error on film in
PQ (Delta E ITP) is 1.36, 1.33 and 1.22 against 1.33 for Lanczos, and
their worst 1 % is lower (8.4, 7.3 and 7.1 against 9.1). They are
offered as they are measured, for those who want them: none is on
unless chosen.

Three expert settings (Player > Videos, level 3): the upscaler for
videos of 1080p and above (1880 wide or 1000 tall or more, so that a
cropped film counts as 1080p), the one for smaller videos, and the one
for animation ("Same as the other two" by default), taken where the
file's genres name animation as a whole genre (Animation, Anime,
Animación and the other names the scrapers give it). Each is Off
(Kodi's scaling, the default), RAVU, FSRCNNX 8 or FSRCNNX 16.

A picture is drawn with one by the bridge (dvbridge_render_set_upscaler())
only where nothing else of the bridge draws it (not native Dolby Vision,
not a Dolby Vision reconstruction), it is drawn large enough for the
shaders to run (their own conditions: more than 1.41 times its size in
width and height for RAVU, 1.3 times for FSRCNNX), progressive, 2D,
with Kodi's brightness and contrast at their middle, and the renderer
can give the bridge its planes (a VAAPI surface of 4:2:0, NV12, P010,
P012 or P016, or the three planes of a 10-bit 4:2:0 picture of the
processor). An HDR picture is drawn so only where its HDR goes out as
HDR; one that the bridge maps (to SDR, or adapted to the display) gets
the upscaler in that mapping, where its shaders run. Everything else
keeps Kodi's way, and the log says why once a video. A change of the
settings, of the size the picture is drawn at, or a failure configures
the renderer again, so a choice applies at once.

It fails closed: an upscaler whose files cannot be read or parsed, one
whose shaders this GPU cannot draw (the bridge then draws the picture
without them), or 48 pictures in a row that could not be drawn turn it
off for the rest of the video with a warning in the log; the pictures
go on as Kodi draws them. Where the GPU time of its pictures averages
85 % of a refresh or more over at least 24 timed pictures (with
GL_EXT_disjoint_timer_query), it is turned off as well and the screen
says so. The log has one line a video saying what draws the video and
why, and every 10 s the pictures drawn with it and their GPU time (the
passes as well, with dvpasstimes in advancedsettings.xml).

The three shaders are shipped as their authors publish them, in
system/hades/upscalers with SOURCES.md (origin, version, SHA256 and how
they were chosen), under LGPL-3.0-or-later (LICENSES). Measured and
left out: FSR 1 (EASU, RCAS: below Lanczos on animation, -1.1 and
-2.5 dB, RCAS -3.6 dB on film in PQ), Anime4K (below Lanczos on
animation scaled 2 times, -0.9 and -1.2 dB, and a lower SSIM than
Lanczos on film), NNEDI3 (its picture comes out one pixel off in
libplacebo's frame), the EWA filters (at most +0.5 dB for 5 to 11
times the time) and ArtCNN (compute shaders: libplacebo's GL backend
has none on GLES).

With the settings off (the default) no picture changes: the bridge is
not given an upscaler and Kodi's renderer takes its usual path.
